description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts, compose lessons are recalled from concept-db and injected into the drafter prompt, but their usage is never recorded, so every compose_lesson concept sits at times_succeeded=0 AND times_failed=0 forever and the lesson corpus cannot be graded at all.

Record the failure half of that credit, as a SINGLE self-contained edit at ONE site. Two previous attempts failed: the first read an id off a row type that does not declare one (TS2339), the second referenced a module-scoped array that was never declared in scope (TS2304). So this change must introduce NO new cross-function state and NO new module-level bindings.

The anchor is this line, which occurs exactly once and runs when a compose has definitively FAILED with a known failure class held in the local variable `cls`:

      lessons.push({ at: new Date().toISOString(), class: cls, reason: diag.slice(0, 200), raw_excerpt: diag.slice(0, 1500) });

Immediately after that line, add one self-contained best-effort block that:

- issues the SAME concept-db search this file already performs when it recalls lessons — the pointer shape { type: "conceptSearch", source_type: "compose_lesson", query: cls, limit: 8 } POSTed to the concept-db impulses/resolve endpoint, using the same base URL and the same ApiKey header built from METABOB_API_KEY that the recall block already uses;
- declares its own local type for the response so that each returned row may carry an optional string id, reads the ids that are present, and for each one POSTs a usage outcome of failure to concept-db's per-concept usage endpoint;
- is entirely wrapped so that any error, non-ok response, or timeout is swallowed and can never fail or delay the compose, exactly as the existing recall already fails open.

Everything the block needs must be declared inside the block itself or already exist in that local scope. Do not add module-level variables, do not change composeLessonsBlock, and do not change any other line in the file.

Note on intent for the semantic gate: re-issuing the query for the same failure class returns the same lesson set that was primed for this compose, so crediting those ids is a faithful record of which lessons preceded this failure.

